  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 311 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nLow amplitude flow across the central CONUS, will persist \nthrough Tuesday night, becoming more amplified beginning \nWednesday. Ridging will amplify across the western third of the \nCONUS, while at the same time, a broad trough of low pressure \nwill deepen across the eastern 2/3rds of the CONUS. As the \ntrough deepens Wednesday into the end of next week, colder air \nwill drop south from eastern Canada into the upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes. This trough will force a back door cold front into \nthe eastern half of Nebraska Wednesday night into Thursday. The \nlatest EC and GFS ensemble forecast has trended highs downward \nfor Thursday and Friday and readings are now below the latest \nNBM ensemble forecast. The WPC forecast tonight initialized with\nupper 50s for highs Thursday and Friday for North Platte which \nis a downward trend from the previous forecast. This seems \nreasonable based on the latest deterministic EC and GFS solns \ntonight and the downward trend in the EC and GFS ensemble \nforecast. Feel the WPC forecast high temps Thursday and Friday \nare a good compromise between the warmest NBM numbers, and \ncoolest MEX statistical guidance. Beyond Friday, the upper level\ntrough across the eastern CONUS, will be forced east as ridging\nover the western CONUS, transitions east into the Rockies and \nhigh plains. With the ridge building into the central CONUS next\nweekend, highs should reach back into the 60s for Saturday and \nSunday (11-14 and 11-15). Dry conditions appear likely for \nSunday nigh through Friday. However, will need to monitor the \npossible threat for light precipitation INVOF the back door \nfront late Wednesday night into Thursday as the latest \ndeterministic GFS currently develops some light QPF across \ncentral portions of Nebraska.",
